{{- define "custom.custom.configuration" -}}
{{ template "custom.custom.configuration.header" . }}

### The Z-Wave controller

**USB stick on a node:** set `device.hostPath` to the stick's
`/dev/serial/by-id/...` path (not `/dev/ttyUSBx`, which can change) and pin
the pod to that node. The stick is mounted at `/dev/zwave`; select that path
under Settings → Z-Wave → Serial port. Device access needs a privileged
container, which the chart enables when `device.hostPath` is set.

```yaml
device:
  hostPath: /dev/serial/by-id/usb-0658_0200-if00

defaultPodOptions:
  nodeSelector:
    kubernetes.io/hostname: node-with-the-stick
```

**Network-attached controller:** leave `device.hostPath` empty and enter
`tcp://host:port` (e.g. ser2net or an Ethernet/Wi-Fi Z-Wave bridge) as the
serial port. The pod then runs unprivileged and can be scheduled anywhere.

### Home Assistant

Enable the WebSocket server under Settings → Home Assistant (port 3000), then
add the Z-Wave integration in Home Assistant with
`ws://<release>.<namespace>.svc:3000`.

### Store and secrets

`persistence.store` holds the settings, the network security keys (S0/S2) and
the node cache. It is enabled by default: losing it means re-including every
device. Back it up.

`SESSION_SECRET` is generated on install and reused on upgrades by reading the
existing Secret (`lookup`). Tools that render with `helm template`, such as
Argo CD, cannot look it up and would create a new one on every sync; set
`sessionSecret` or `existingSecret` there.
{{- end -}}
